Absolute Vs Relative Vs internal

<!-- Notes: Difference between Absolute, Relative, and Internal Links

1️⃣ Absolute Links
------------------
→ Used to connect to external websites (outside your project).
→ Always include the full URL (with https:// or http://).
→ Example: <a href="https://www.google.com">Google</a>

💡 Points:
- Opens a page from another domain.
- Useful for social links, references, or external sources.
- If you move your website to another domain, these links still work.
- You can use target="_blank" to open them in a new tab.

------------------------------------------------------------

2️⃣ Relative Links
------------------
→ Used to connect pages within the same website or project folder.
→ Uses a file path instead of the full web address.

Examples:
- <a href="about.html">About</a> (same folder)
- <a href="pages/contact.html">Contact</a> (subfolder)
- <a href="../index.html">Home</a> (parent folder)

💡 Points:
- Depends on the folder structure of your project.
- Makes your website portable — links still work even if moved to a new domain.
- Used only for internal navigation (within your website).

------------------------------------------------------------

3️⃣ Internal (Anchor) Links
----------------------------
→ Used to jump to a specific section within the same page.

Examples:
- Add an id: <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
- Create a link: <a href="#contact">Go to Contact</a>

💡 Points:
- Used for “Back to Top” links or navigation on long pages.
- Helps improve user experience and accessibility.
- You can also link to an element on another page:
  <a href="about.html#team">Meet the Team</a>

------------------------------------------------------------

